1
00:00:00,007 --> 00:00:05,460
How can the observable universe
be 93 billion light-years across

2
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:09,680
if it is only 13.8 billion years old?

3
00:00:10,776 --> 00:00:12,580
If light travels at a constant speed,

4
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:16,980
this vast cosmic scale sounds
like an impossible contradiction.

5
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:21,820
So why does this cosmic puzzle matter
so much when we look out into space?

6
00:00:22,931 --> 00:00:28,360
Objects beyond this physical boundary cannot
be detected yet because their light has

7
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:30,140
not had enough time to reach Earth.

8
00:00:30,831 --> 00:00:35,920
In this video, we will answer three
fundamental questions about our cosmic horizon

9
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:36,960
in plain words.

10
00:00:37,773 --> 00:00:41,660
How does space stretch while light is
actively traveling across the universe?

11
00:00:42,850 --> 00:00:48,180
How does a travel time of 13.8 billion
years convert into a physical radius

12
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:51,576
of 46.5 billion light-years?

13
00:00:52,434 --> 00:00:56,940
And what mysterious regions lie forever
beyond the edge of our current observable

14
00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:57,740
cosmos?

15
00:00:58,853 --> 00:01:03,560
To solve this mystery, we must reframe
how we imagine cosmic space and time.

16
00:01:05,229 --> 00:01:10,260
Many people visualize the universe as
a huge empty room where galaxies rocket

17
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:11,300
through a dark void.

18
00:01:12,106 --> 00:01:15,760
In that false mental picture,
galaxies are racing through a fixed,

19
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:18,200
static space like cosmic shrapnel.

20
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:23,980
If that picture were correct, nothing
could ever appear farther away than 13.8

21
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:25,324
billion light-years.

22
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:30,920
But this common picture of cosmic
expansion is completely wrong.

23
00:01:31,713 --> 00:01:36,560
Cosmic expansion is not galaxies moving
through a pre-existing empty space.

24
00:01:37,326 --> 00:01:42,620
Instead, general relativity describes
expansion as an intrinsic increase in

25
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:45,600
distance between cosmic objects over time.

26
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:50,840
Most galaxies are nearly at rest
relative to their local comoving spatial

27
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:51,640
coordinates.

28
00:01:52,299 --> 00:01:57,480
The velocity of a galaxy relative to its
local comoving grid is called its peculiar

29
00:01:57,480 --> 00:01:58,280
velocity.

30
00:01:58,942 --> 00:02:04,060
These local peculiar velocities are caused
mostly by gravity and are usually quite

31
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:04,860
small.

32
00:02:05,192 --> 00:02:09,000
So how should we picture the movement
of galaxies as the universe expands?

33
00:02:10,234 --> 00:02:14,980
Understanding this key distinction changes
everything about how we calculate cosmic

34
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:15,780
distances.

35
00:02:17,513 --> 00:02:19,180
To understand cosmic distances,

36
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:24,220
imagine space as an expanding
fabric rather than a rigid stage.

37
00:02:24,888 --> 00:02:29,860
Mathematically, cosmologists track this
growth using a value called the cosmic scale

38
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:30,660
factor.

39
00:02:31,019 --> 00:02:33,020
The scale factor grows over time,

40
00:02:33,340 --> 00:02:37,100
describing how physical distances
expand across the universe.

41
00:02:37,867 --> 00:02:39,440
In the early history of the universe,

42
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:42,060
the scale factor was extremely small.

43
00:02:42,816 --> 00:02:46,920
That means all matter in the young cosmos
was squeezed much closer together.

44
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:52,400
Before recombination, the universe was
filled with an opaque white-hot fog of

45
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:53,180
plasma.

46
00:02:53,470 --> 00:02:58,660
Once neutral atoms formed, ancient photons
became free to travel freely through

47
00:02:58,660 --> 00:02:59,620
transparent space.

48
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:05,880
These released photons created the cosmic
microwave background radiation that fills

49
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:06,680
space today.

50
00:03:07,345 --> 00:03:09,020
When this ancient light was first emitted,

51
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:12,920
its source was only 42 million
light-years from Earth.

52
00:03:13,652 --> 00:03:18,900
Over 13.8 billion years,
continuous expansion stretched

53
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:20,460
that distance dramatically.

54
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:26,680
How does a light wave move through space
that expands while the light is moving?

55
00:03:27,799 --> 00:03:31,780
Imagine an ant crawling along a
stretching rubber band toward your hand.

56
00:03:32,493 --> 00:03:37,160
The ant crawls forward at a steady pace
relative to the rubber directly under its

57
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:37,960
feet.

58
00:03:38,043 --> 00:03:42,980
Similarly, a photon always travels through
space locally at the exact speed of

59
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:43,780
light.

60
00:03:43,863 --> 00:03:48,440
A local comoving observer will always
measure the speed of light as the standard

61
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:49,240
constant speed.

62
00:03:50,021 --> 00:03:51,600
But while the photon travels forward,

63
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,040
the space behind it continues
to expand over time.

64
00:03:55,734 --> 00:03:59,300
At the same time, the space ahead
of the photon stretches as well.

65
00:04:00,053 --> 00:04:01,420
Because space stretches everywhere,

66
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:05,500
the distance between the photon
and its source grows continuously.

67
00:04:06,473 --> 00:04:11,820
Photons from the cosmic microwave
background spent 13.8 billion years

68
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:14,140
traveling across space to reach Earth.

69
00:04:14,847 --> 00:04:17,340
But during those 13.8 billion years,

70
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:21,900
expansion pushed the matter that
emitted those photons much farther away.

71
00:04:23,603 --> 00:04:28,860
So, how far away is that emitting matter
located at the present cosmological time?

72
00:04:29,908 --> 00:04:34,420
To understand this, we must distinguish
between light-travel distance and proper

73
00:04:34,420 --> 00:04:35,220
distance.

74
00:04:35,688 --> 00:04:40,840
Light-travel distance is simply the age of
the universe multiplied by the speed of

75
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:41,640
light.

76
00:04:41,781 --> 00:04:46,620
That calculation yields a light-travel
distance of about 13.8 billion

77
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:47,638
light-years.

78
00:04:48,084 --> 00:04:53,500
Proper distance represents the actual
physical distance between two objects at

79
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:54,860
a single moment in time.

80
00:04:55,532 --> 00:05:00,320
You can imagine proper distance as the
value measured by rulers if expansion

81
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:01,200
suddenly froze.

82
00:05:02,068 --> 00:05:07,420
Comoving distance factors out expansion
and equals proper distance at the present

83
00:05:07,420 --> 00:05:08,480
age of the universe.

84
00:05:09,303 --> 00:05:14,600
From Earth, the comoving distance to the
edge of our observable sphere is about

85
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:18,428
46.5 billion light-years.

86
00:05:18,871 --> 00:05:21,680
This radial distance marks
our particle horizon,

87
00:05:22,210 --> 00:05:24,340
which limits what signals can reach us.

88
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:30,480
Thus, our observable universe is a
sphere with a radius of 46.5 billion

89
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:31,396
light-years.

90
00:05:32,837 --> 00:05:35,040
Because Earth receives
light from all directions,

91
00:05:35,820 --> 00:05:38,540
our observable horizon
forms a massive sphere.

92
00:05:39,248 --> 00:05:44,400
Every point in the universe has its own
distinct observable sphere centered on its

93
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:45,200
location.

94
00:05:45,701 --> 00:05:48,300
To find the diameter of
Earth's observable sphere,

95
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:52,640
we double the 46.5
billion light-year radius.

96
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:58,980
Doubling that radius yields
a full cosmic diameter of 93

97
00:05:58,980 --> 00:06:01,040
billion light-years across.

98
00:06:01,854 --> 00:06:06,600
That total count means there are more
stars in our view than beach sand grains on

99
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:07,400
Earth.

100
00:06:07,555 --> 00:06:09,440
This ordinary matter includes stars,

101
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:13,200
planets, interstellar gas,
and intergalactic gas,

102
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:15,060
but excludes dark matter.

103
00:06:15,752 --> 00:06:20,440
Every object within this sphere is visible
because its light had time to travel to

104
00:06:20,440 --> 00:06:21,240
Earth.

105
00:06:22,430 --> 00:06:27,520
So, how can the observable
universe be 93 billion light-years

106
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:31,720
wide after only 13.8 billion years?

107
00:06:32,929 --> 00:06:37,780
Space itself stretched continuously while
light made its long journey across the

108
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:38,700
expanding universe.

109
00:06:39,496 --> 00:06:44,900
That cosmic expansion turned 13.8
billion years of light travel into a

110
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:48,480
46.5 billion light-year physical radius.

111
00:06:49,272 --> 00:06:54,420
Doubling that radius gives the full
observable diameter of 93 billion

112
00:06:54,420 --> 00:06:55,400
light-years today.

113
00:06:56,100 --> 00:07:01,100
Objects located beyond our particle
horizon have emitted light that has not yet

114
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:01,900
reached Earth.

115
00:07:02,460 --> 00:07:06,360
The unobservable portion of the
entire universe may be far larger,

116
00:07:06,840 --> 00:07:08,460
or even infinite in size.

117
00:07:09,318 --> 00:07:13,920
Furthermore, dark energy is currently
driving an accelerated expansion of space.

118
00:07:14,693 --> 00:07:16,440
Because expansion accelerates,

119
00:07:16,820 --> 00:07:21,883
astronomers calculate a future visibility
limit at 62 billion light-years

120
00:07:21,883 --> 00:07:22,780
comoving distance.

121
00:07:24,109 --> 00:07:29,400
Light emitted today by objects
farther than 16 billion light-years

122
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:32,900
will never reach us in
the infinite future.

123
00:07:33,604 --> 00:07:38,260
Distant galaxies outside our local
supercluster will eventually appear to freeze,

124
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:40,360
turn redder, and fade away.

125
00:07:41,048 --> 00:07:46,520
Take a moment to think about how much of
our universe will remain forever beyond

126
00:07:46,520 --> 00:07:47,320
your reach.
